<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoleonic Code</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Napoleonic Code is the French civil code issued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1804, codifying law on persons, property and contracts. It was exported across continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">First Empire</a>'s period of dominance. Through European states and their colonies it shaped legal systems in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, one of the French Empire's most enduring legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Code" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DerHexer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Speyer,_Historical_Museum_of_the_Palatinate_-_Code_civil_des_Fran%C3%A7ais.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, exported across continental Europe during Napoleon's brief continental dominance, fundamentally shaped the legal systems of France's European neighbours and, through them and through French colonial rule, those of states across Africa and the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution of 1987, amended in 2012, made a semi-presidential republic: a president elected for five years without consecutive terms, a prime minister from the majority in the National Assembly, a two-chamber National Assembly and a modified <b>Napoleonic Code</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Boyer centralised government in Port-au-Prince, issued a civil code modelled on the <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, and relied on army commanders who ran each district and on a small circle of mixed-race officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Napoleon, ruling by force, carried the civil equality and secular law of the <b>Napoleonic Code</b> of 1804 across much of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman penal code of 1858, modelled on France’s <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, abolished punishments such as amputation of a limb for theft and decriminalized homosexuality; it endured until 1928.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1807 abolished personal serfdom, declared equality before the law and introduced the <b>Napoleonic Code</b>, though nobles kept their land.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-warsaw">Duchy of Warsaw</a></li></ul></section>
